U   U1  in      xxx

R   R   in      gnd
C   C   gnd     xxx

/* Main result is the transfer function from input to output voltage. We reference the
   output voltage by implicit name, U_ followed by the name of a node. */
PLOT G U_gnd U1
RES R U1 // Fails, too


/* Caution, this file produces an assertion:
Jun 12 16:18:54 2026 - 000010 ms - RESULT - Reading circuit file rc1.cnl successfully done
Assertion failed: !success || !storeDet, file code/linNet/sol_solver.c, line 924

The assertion came after renaming node out into gnd. We have an error in the PLOT
statement, U_out is no longer defined after renaming, but this is not the root cause.
(Modifying the PLOT statement into "PLOT G U_gnd U1" produces the same assertion.)

Background of renaming was demonstrating how the choice of the ground node impacts the
calculation result. In the original circuit, node xxx used to be gnd and gnd used to be
out.

Root cause seems to be sol_solver.c, getVectorOfReqDependents(), l. 106, which recognizes
the problem of bad potential references, but doesn't report it. Idea is to first calculate
everything else so that most of the user results will still be available. We need to filter
only the extreme situation, when this strategy leads to an empty set of remaining
calculations. (It's probably not possible to just remove the assertion and go on; the
solution object doesn't have a determinante and subsequent errors may result - this to be
checked.)

Besides the assertion, this revealed another issue: The result depends on the gnd node but
without expcilitly naming a node gnd, linNet makes the choice itself but doesn't warn about
it. Actually, without specifying the ground node but still specifying ratios of node
voltages as 
results, these results are undefined: Numerator and denonminator have an undefined offset
(the delta voltage between expected and actual ground node)
and the ratio depends on that offset. Not the calculation is wrong but the user's
expectations. We need to make clear that the user didn't fully specify his demands.

The problem shouldn't occur with results, which depend only on fully defined user voltages.
In all other cases, if there is at least one other result, linNet should emit a warning if
no ground node is specified. However, it'll be difficult to categorize the results
accordingly. The cheap way out would be a warning, whichis always emitted when it comes to
automatic ground node selection. Probably, we already have an according message on DEBUG or
INFO level.

The message:
INFO - Node gnd is considered a ground node. The voltage at this node is considered null 
can be considered a part of the result. Is it sufficient o make this level RESULT? Wouldn't
it important to bring this into the Octave files, too? (les_linearEquationSystem.c, l. 960)

Once the assertion is fixed, we can make this test case a threefold one, with different
ground nodes selected and comparing the results.

Meanwhile: A fix is available as branch issues/1-bad-result-def-asserts.
*/